(CLI-04: `--shared` - build a shared library (.so) instead of an
 executable. Also emits a companion `.lib` interface file next to the
 `.so` (undocumented in this section, but needed by CLI-05/CLI-06's
 `--link`/`--lib-path` and by the Libraries and Imports chapter's `see
 ... from "....lib"` consumption path).
 RE-VERIFIED 2026-08-22 against vox 0.4.9: this file used to also be an
 ordinary compilable program on its own (calling 'double it' at the top
 level and printing the result, so one source served both roles). That
 dual-purpose design is now BROKEN -- the manual's Libraries and
 Imports chapter now documents (LANGUAGE.md:5204-5211, libraries.md
 LIB-25, already hand-verified there) that a `--shared` compile rejects
 ANY top-level executable statement: "Top-level ... statement is not
 allowed in a shared library: only function definitions, 'Library', and
 'see' may appear at the top level." The old two-statement tail below
 made `--shared -o libtally.so` fail outright with that exact error as
 of 0.4.9, even though the identical file still compiled and ran fine
 (printing 42) as an ordinary program. Stripped the tail so this file
 is --shared-clean; `--shared` really is a stricter dialect now, not
 just "a build mode" as originally claimed here -- that framing is
 retired, cross-ref LIB-25 for the rule itself.
 Hand-run separately:
   $ vox tally_lib.vox --shared -o libtally.so
   $ ls libtally.so libtally.lib
   libtally.lib
   libtally.so
   $ file libtally.so
   libtally.so: ELF 64-bit LSB shared object, x86-64, ...
 Ran: (not runnable as a single ordinary-compile file any more -- see
 the note above; --shared transcript above is the verification)
)
Library tally version "1.0".

To 'double it' with a number called value. Return a number, value multiply 2.
